# UI Slice 10's verification: three new painter primitives, on screen.
#
# The claim is that a chart became writable in a *consumer* — stroked polylines
# for the curves, filled convex polygons for the area under one of them, and a
# textured quad for a thumbnail the demo shaded itself — with no widget added to
# `slmsttaa-ui`, and with the overlay still a single `draw_indexed`. Three of
# those are pictures, and these are the pictures.
#
# Run it:  cargo xtask shoot terrain --script capture/terrain-plot.script
#
# Frame numbers, not seconds. The engine holds on each one, so a click lands on a
# known frame and the erosion is at a known pass — which is what makes the
# coordinates below stable rather than a guess about timing. At the default 8
# passes a second, frame N is pass N/7.5, and at the default 512² grid the bake
# runs to pass 75 and stops on its own at about frame 562.
#
# Two things about the coordinates, both learned the hard way elsewhere:
#
#  - **They were read off `shot rest`**, in physical pixels from the window's
#    top-left. The pause button is at (97, 109); the Plot heading at (52, 250).
#    The Bake section holds that second number still on purpose: every row in it
#    is drawn unconditionally, so the panel does not reflow when the bake
#    finishes and these coordinates mean the same thing at every frame below.
#  - **Every click here is on or above the Plot heading**, deliberately.
#    Collapsing a section reflows only the rows beneath it, so nothing in this
#    file moves under its own steps. That is the reflow trap
#    `capture/editor-list.script` records, sidestepped by ordering rather than
#    survived.
#
# This file used to have to *pause* the demo before its last two shots, because
# the timeline ran forever and a moving landscape cannot be diffed. It does not
# any more: the bake reaches its last pass and stops, so the world is already
# still.
#
# It also used to be shaped by a limit that has since gone: `click` alone cannot
# move a slider (it delivers both edges inside one frozen frame, so
# `Response::held` is never true), so this file reached the pass it wanted by
# letting the demo *play* to it rather than by scrubbing. The harness now has
# `press` and `release` as separate steps — see `capture/terrain-drag.script` —
# but the playing route is kept here, because it is the shorter script and the
# thing this file is about is the plot, not the transport.

# Frame 200: about pass 26. Lakes still standing inland, both curves climbing
# away from the origin, and the minimap wet. The reference shot, and where the
# coordinates above were read from.
200  shot  rest

# Frame 400: about pass 53, still baking. The curves have grown to the right and
# the marker with them; the area fill under lake depth has grown with both. Two
# shots of a plot that is being *made* is the cheapest proof the marker is a
# function of the pass rather than a decoration.
400  shot  mid

# Frame 620: past the end of the bake. Lake depth has decayed to nearly nothing
# while height-moved has not, which is the demo's own headless measurement —
# "lake coverage falls to zero" — finally drawn. The minimap has lost its inland
# water at the same time, from the same data, which is what says the image and
# the plot are two views of one thing rather than two pictures that happen to
# agree. The ocean is still there, because an ocean is base level and not a lake.
620  shot  late

# Collapse the Plot heading. Everything below it moves up and the panel shrinks,
# which proves the section is an ordinary `ui.section` with no privileges — and
# leaves a picture of the demo with the capability switched off to diff against.
660  move  52 250
680  click
720  shot  collapsed
